TI  - Impaired Respiratory Health and Life Course Transitions From Health to Chronic 
      Lung Disease.
PG  - 879-889
LID - S0012-3692(21)00695-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2021.04.009 [doi]
AB  - Primary prevention and interception of chronic lung disease are essential in the 
      effort to reduce the morbidity and mortality caused by respiratory conditions. In 
      this review, we apply a life course approach that examines exposures across the 
      life span to identify risk factors that are associated with not only chronic lung 
      disease but also an intermediate phenotype between ideal lung health and lung 
      disease, termed "impaired respiratory health." Notably, risk factors such as 
      exposure to tobacco smoke and air pollution, as well as obesity and physical 
      fitness, affect respiratory health across the life course by being associated 
      with both abnormal lung growth and lung function decline. We then discuss the 
      importance of disease interception and identifying those at highest risk of 
      developing chronic lung disease. This work begins with understanding and 
      detecting impaired respiratory health, and we review several promising molecular 
      biomarkers, predictive symptoms, and early imaging findings that may lead to a 
      better understanding of this intermediate phenotype.
