LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2020.09.264 [doi]
AB  - A 69-year-old woman with a medical history significant for COPD, ulcerative 
      colitis (UC), and tobacco dependence was referred to a pulmonologist for lung 
      nodules found on routine annual low-dose CT scan for lung cancer screening. Her 
      review of systems was negative for dyspnea, angina, hemoptysis, fever, night 
      sweats, anorexia, and weight loss. She had a successful total proctocolectomy 
      with ileal pouch-anal anastomosis performed 5 years ago because of acute 
      fulminant UC refractory to corticosteroids and biologic agents. Her home 
      medications were albuterol inhaler, umeclidinium, and vilanterol inhalation 
      powder. She denied any history of lung cancers in her family. She was an active 
      smoker and had a 35-pack-year smoking history. She worked as a cashier in a local 
      supermarket and had been doing so for the past 25 years.
