TI  - A 60-Year-Old Man With a Migratory Lobar Consolidation After Invasive 
      Ventilation.
PG  - e163-e166
LID - S0012-3692(22)04160-5 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2022.11.012 [doi]
AB  - A 60-year-old man was referred to a pulmonologist evaluation with persistent 
      dyspnea and cough 1 month after discharge for an acute respiratory failure caused 
      by Legionella pneumophila pneumonia, which required invasive mechanical 
      ventilation. Chest CT scan performed during hospitalization showed lobar 
      consolidation of upper left lobe (Fig 1A). Bronchial culture revealed L 
      pneumophila; the patient was treated with levofloxacin and supported with 
      invasive mechanical ventilation for 2 weeks. Chest radiograph after extubation 
      showed almost complete resolution of infiltrates. After 1 month, the patient 
      still complained of dyspnea and a new chest CT scan was performed: the 
      consolidation migrated (Fig 1B; Video 1).
