TI  - A 16-Year-Old-Girl With Asthma, Palpable Purpura, and Hypereosinophilia.
PG  - e305-e308
LID - S0012-3692(21)05079-0 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2021.12.632 [doi]
AB  - A 16-year-old girl presented to the ED with complaints of loose stools, abdominal 
      pain, and rash over her legs for the last 7 days. There was no history of fever, 
      vomiting, oral ulcers, or mucosal bleeds. In the past, she had received a 
      diagnosis of asthma. She had been taking oral montelukast every day for the past 
      year and using a formoterol-budesonide dry powder inhaler irregularly, only 
      during episodes of acute exacerbations. There was a history of significant but 
      undocumented weight loss. On day 3 of hospital admission, she developed numbness 
      over her right foot.
