LID - S0012-3692(20)35339-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2020.11.047 [doi]
AB  - A 54-year-old man sought treatment at the ED for a productive cough with green 
      phlegm of approximately 6 months' duration that was accompanied by a 10-pound 
      weight loss, night sweats, and occasional subjective fevers. He had made several 
      prior visits to the ED for the cough and was hospitalized 4 months earlier for 
      similar symptoms, at which time he underwent a bronchoscopy with BAL and was 
      discharged with antibiotics for presumed pneumonia. He did not report any 
      itching, rashes, sinus infections, joint swelling, joint pain, or GI symptoms. 
      His long-term medications included omeprazole and amlodipine. The patient had a 
      past medical history of grade III follicular lymphoma for which he completed six 
      cycles of bendamustine 4 years before presentation and had been in remission 
      since. He was a never smoker, had a recent travel history to the Dominican 
      Republic 8 months before admission, and had no recent sick contacts.
