TI  - Laryngeal Injury Due to Amikacin Inhalation for Refractory Mycobacterium avium 
      Complex Infection.
PG  - e185-e187
LID - S0012-3692(20)35337-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2020.11.045 [doi]
AB  - Inhaled antibiotics have long been used for chronic lung infections, especially 
      in patients with cystic fibrosis and increasingly for non-cystic fibrosis 
      bronchiectasis. Amikacin liposome inhalation suspension (ALIS) has emerged as a 
      promising treatment for Mycobacterium avium complex infection refractory to oral 
      antibiotics. However, despite its efficacy, nearly one-half of patients in phase 
      II and III trials experienced dysphonia as a treatment-associated adverse effect. 
      Here, we describe a patient who experienced severe, acute-onset laryngitis while 
      receiving ALIS for refractory M avium complex infection, prompting 
      discontinuation of ALIS therapy. This is the first report directly describing 
      vocal fold injury due to such therapy. Given the high frequency of dysphonia 
      reported with ALIS, this case highlights the potential severity of laryngeal 
      toxicity, the importance of coordination of care for patients receiving inhaled 
      antibiotics for chronic pulmonary disease, and the need for better insight into 
      mechanisms of toxicity.
