TI  - A 56-Year-Old Man With Cough, Bloody Sputum, and Lithoptysis.
PG  - e255-e258
LID - S0012-3692(23)00002-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2022.12.025 [doi]
AB  - A 56-year-old Chinese man, who did not smoke, presented with a 2-month history of 
      cough and bloody sputum. He also complained of fatigue, night sweats, chest pain, 
      and shortness of breath, with no chills or loss of weight. He previously worked 
      as a veterinarian and had been infected with Brucella 30 years ago. Additionally, 
      he had been diagnosed with tuberculous pleurisy and completed a 1-year anti-TB 
      treatment. Subsequently, he had been well until 2 months before the current 
      admission. A chest CT scan showed a cruciform calcification in the mediastinum 
      and some tree-in-bud changes. The results of the purified protein derivative skin 
      test and interferon-gamma release assay for TB were negative. Brucella 
      agglutination test was also negative. On the night of admission, the patient 
      coughed up two silver-white-colored shiny stones and had a fever of up to 38.5 °C 
      on the following days.
