TI  - An Adolescent With a Retropharyngeal Swelling: To Drain or Not to Drain?: Keeping 
      a Broad Vision During the COVID-19 Pandemic.
PG  - e89-e92
LID - S0012-3692(22)00420-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2022.03.001 [doi]
AB  - An 18-year-old patient with a history of COVID-19 (1 month previously) was 
      admitted with malaise and complaints of a stiff neck, a left-sided cervical mass, 
      headache, and difficulty in swallowing and breathing, which had been present for 
      4 days. Two days after the onset of the first symptoms, a painless skin rash on 
      the legs, arms, palms of both hands, and soles of both feet developed. Despite 
      2 days of treatment with antibiotics (amoxicillin/clavulanic acid, 500/125 mg 
      three times daily orally), symptoms progressed. On presentation, the patient was 
      alert and oriented, there were no neurologic disorders, and all symptoms related 
      to the recent COVID-19 infection had subsided. His medical history was negative 
      for sexually transmitted diseases, and the patient had received all vaccines 
      except for meningococcus and COVID-19.
