TI  - A 62-Year-Old Woman With Nodules, Cysts, and Activated Bone Marrow.
PG  - e175-e180
LID - S0012-3692(21)04209-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2021.10.022 [doi]
AB  - A 62-year-old woman with a long-term smoking history was evaluated at our lung 
      cancer clinic for a new 2.5-cm lung nodule. She had a history of well-controlled 
      COPD and hypertension. She was in overall good health until 3 weeks before her 
      evaluation in an ED for new-onset exertional dyspnea. Her physical examination 
      was unremarkable, except for diffuse hyperpigmented scaly scalp lesions that 
      coalesced into plaques. Her subjective symptoms were nonproductive cough, 
      exertional dyspnea, unintentional weight loss of 10 lb, and fatigue that had 
      started 2 months earlier. She did not have fever or night sweats.
