TI  - Recurrent Massive Hemothorax of Unknown Etiology in an 85-Year-Old Man.
PG  - e103-e110
LID - S0012-3692(21)03912-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2021.09.012 [doi]
AB  - An 85-year-old Japanese man, who was taking aspirin and edoxaban for previous 
      myocardial infarction and atrial fibrillation, came to our hospital with a chief 
      complaint of dyspnea for 3 weeks. Chest radiography showed a massive left pleural 
      effusion (Fig 1A). Analysis of pleural fluid showed an elevated hematocrit level 
      at 32.8% (blood hematocrit level, 32.0%), and he was diagnosed with hemothorax. 
      However, he had neither coagulation disorder nor thrombocytopenia, and the 
      pleural effusion was negative for atypical cells. These findings suggested that 
      the antithrombotic and anticoagulant medications might have induced the 
      hemothorax.
