TI  - Dyspnea, Elevated Jugular Venous Pulse, and Lower Extremity Edema in the Setting 
      of Interstitial Lung Disease in a Patient Who Has Undergone Liver and Kidney 
      Transplantation.
PG  - e295-e298
LID - S0012-3692(21)00664-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2021.03.051 [doi]
AB  - A 64-year-old man with a past medical history of alcoholic cirrhosis with 
      resultant hepatorenal syndrome requiring kidney and liver transplantation 10 
      years previously sought treatment at the ED with progressive lower-extremity 
      edema and dyspnea. After noting worsening shortness of breath and cough as an 
      outpatient, he had been referred to a pulmonary clinic and was undergoing a 
      workup for interstitial lung disease (ILD). He had been started on prednisone 
      40 mg/d after a lung biopsy 4 months before admission. He was also receiving 
      chronic immunosuppression with tacrolimus and mycophenolate mofetil. He had noted 
      worsening of edema since starting prednisone.
