TI  - A 20-Year-Old Woman With Thoracic Pain and Progressive Dyspnea on Exertion.
PG  - e233-e240
LID - S0012-3692(21)04378-6 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2021.11.011 [doi]
AB  - A 20-year-old woman presented with dry cough, right-sided thoracic pain, and 
      gradually progressive dyspnea on exertion. She had no hemoptysis or fever. There 
      was no relevant medical history. She was a never smoker and used no medication 
      besides oral contraceptives. There were no other risk factors for a pulmonary 
      embolism. There was a family history of ovarian and breast cancer. Physical 
      examination showed a mildly ill-looking woman, with shallow breathing and normal 
      blood oxygen saturation. Auscultation revealed normal breath sounds without 
      crackles or wheezing. Laboratory testing showed a significantly increased D-dimer 
      (4,560 μg/L [normal, < 500 μg/L]), elevated C-reactive protein (131 mg/L 
      [normal, < 5 mg/L]), normal leucocytes, and elevated lactate dehydrogenase (825 
      units/L [normal, 50 to 250 units/L).
