TI  - A 47-Year-Old Woman With Pulmonary Nodules and Facial Hemispasms.
PG  - e197-e204
LID - S0012-3692(20)30855-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2020.03.078 [doi]
AB  - A 47-year-old woman visited her primary physician for a health check, and some 
      radiographic abnormalities were detected. She was referred to our division for 
      further management. In recent years, she had become conscious of occasional 
      facial hemispasms. She denied respiratory symptoms, smoking, alcohol consumption, 
      and any particle inhalation. She had undergone oophorectomy and platinum-based 
      chemotherapy because of ovarian cancer (serous cystadenocarcinoma stage Ⅰa) at 
      the age of 29 years, with no recurrence for 17 years. The patient was diagnosed 
      with rheumatoid arthritis (RA) 5 years before being seen by us and had been 
      treated with bucillamine. No signs of RA progression were evident, and the only 
      used antirheumatic drug was bucillamine. The patient had no history of use of 
      immune-modulating drugs or immunosuppressants. No previous chest radiographs or 
      CT had been performed.
