TI  - A 33-Year-Old Man With Shortness of Breath, Leukocytosis, and Intermittent 
      Fevers.
PG  - e175-e179
LID - S0012-3692(20)31634-2 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2020.05.567 [doi]
AB  - A 33-year-old man with ulcerative colitis (UC) and primary sclerosing cholangitis 
      presented with worsening shortness of breath, nonproductive cough, and 
      intermittent fevers after he was found to have a WBC count of 27,000 cells/μL on 
      an outpatient laboratory evaluation. He reported feeling progressively unwell 
      with intermittent right upper quadrant pain and shortness of breath since a 
      hospital admission for a UC flare 6 months prior, during which he was first 
      diagnosed with primary sclerosing cholangitis. He noted that prior to that 
      admission 6 months ago, his UC had been in remission for > 10 years. He reported 
      fevers up to 38.9°C on and off for several weeks but was afebrile (37.2°C) on 
      presentation. He endorsed non-bloody diarrhea, chills, night sweats, leg 
      swelling, and associated leg pain. He had a cough but denied sputum production. 
      He reported no recent travels and denied sick contacts. His medications included 
      mesalamine, ursodiol, montelukast, and an albuterol inhaler.
