TI  - Cross-Sectional Studies: Strengths, Weaknesses, and Recommendations.
PG  - S65-S71
LID - S0012-3692(20)30462-1 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.chest.2020.03.012 [doi]
AB  - Cross-sectional studies are observational studies that analyze data from a 
      population at a single point in time. They are often used to measure the 
      prevalence of health outcomes, understand determinants of health, and describe 
      features of a population. Unlike other types of observational studies, 
      cross-sectional studies do not follow individuals up over time. They are usually 
      inexpensive and easy to conduct. They are useful for establishing preliminary 
      evidence in planning a future advanced study. This article reviews the essential 
      characteristics, describes strengths and weaknesses, discusses methodological 
      issues, and gives our recommendations on design and statistical analysis for 
      cross-sectional studies in pulmonary and critical care medicine. A list of 
      considerations for reviewers is also provided.
